WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.870
Every box that comes into the storeroom passes the clerk at the door.

2
00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:12.491
The clerk reads its label, box of apples, checks that only apples go in, and nods it through.

3
00:00:12.491 --> 00:00:16.117
Then, as you've heard before, the clerk tears the label off.

4
00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:20.399
On the shelves, a box of apples and a box of lemons look alike.

5
00:00:20.399 --> 00:00:24.465
That's fine, until someone asks a box: are you a box of apples?

6
00:00:24.465 --> 00:00:26.401
There's no label left to read.

7
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:30.365
In code, that tearing off is called type erasure.

8
00:00:30.365 --> 00:00:35.563
The compiler checks every type argument, then leaves it out of the compiled program.

9
00:00:35.563 --> 00:00:39.895
At run time, a list of names and a list of weights are the same class.

10
00:00:39.895 --> 00:00:42.741
The demo prints: same class at run time, true.

11
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:45.565
So a type check can only test the class.

12
00:00:45.565 --> 00:00:51.995
Ask whether something is a List&lt;String&gt;, and Kotlin says: cannot check for instance of erased type.

13
00:00:51.995 --> 00:00:57.451
Ask whether it is T, inside an ordinary generic function, and it fails the same way.

14
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:01.404
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

15
00:01:01.404 --> 00:01:05.762
When I call isA&lt;Apple&gt;, I typed Apple right there, in plain sight.

16
00:01:05.762 --> 00:01:07.083
The compiler saw it.

17
00:01:07.083 --> 00:01:08.337
So where did it go?

18
00:01:08.337 --> 00:01:11.243
Why can't my function just ask T what it is?

19
00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:14.464
Picture a second clerk at the door.

20
00:01:14.464 --> 00:01:17.386
This one knows the labels are about to come off.

21
00:01:17.386 --> 00:01:23.718
So before a label goes, the clerk copies it onto a note, and pins the note to the job: this job, apples.

22
00:01:23.718 --> 00:01:28.040
When the job reaches the shop floor, whoever does it can read the note.

23
00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:31.898
Kotlin's version of that clerk has two parts.

24
00:01:31.898 --> 00:01:33.201
The first is inline.

25
00:01:33.201 --> 00:01:38.738
Normally a function has one shared body, compiled once, and every caller jumps to it.

26
00:01:38.738 --> 00:01:43.884
Mark it inline, and the compiler copies the body into each place that calls it.

27
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.654
And at each of those places, the type argument is known.

28
00:01:47.654 --> 00:01:52.732
A call like isA&lt;Apple&gt; is being compiled right there, with Apple in plain sight.

29
00:01:52.933 --> 00:01:54.696
The second part is reified.

30
00:01:54.696 --> 00:01:59.463
It sounds like something a doctor would say, but it just means made real.

31
00:01:59.463 --> 00:02:05.014
Mark T reified, and the compiler writes the caller's real class into the copied body.

32
00:02:05.014 --> 00:02:06.059
That's the note.

33
00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:07.779
Here it is.

34
00:02:07.779 --> 00:02:13.684
isA is an inline function with a reified T, and its body is one line: this is T.

35
00:02:13.684 --> 00:02:20.107
Java has no such clerk, so it passes the class by hand: a Class object, and isInstance.

36
00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:23.381
Now watch what happens to the call.

37
00:02:23.381 --> 00:02:26.199
gala dot isA&lt;Apple&gt; isn't a call any more.

38
00:02:26.199 --> 00:02:30.090
The body is copied into the caller, with Apple written in.

39
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.585
In the caller's own bytecode, it has become: gala instanceof Apple.

40
00:02:34.585 --> 00:02:36.597
A real check, on a real class.

41
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:43.002
So let's sort a mixed delivery: two apples, a lemon, a paper bag, and a null.

42
00:02:43.002 --> 00:02:44.216
Is Gala an apple?

43
00:02:44.216 --> 00:02:45.002
Is the bag?

44
00:02:45.002 --> 00:02:49.575
And filterIsInstance, from the standard library, is reified too.

45
00:02:49.575 --> 00:02:50.861
What does it keep?

46
00:02:50.861 --> 00:02:52.004
Pause and guess.

47
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.305
The demo prints: Gala isA&lt;Apple&gt;, true.

48
00:02:58.305 --> 00:02:59.499
The bag, false.

49
00:02:59.499 --> 00:03:01.250
Apples: Gala and Fuji.

50
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:02.444
Lemons: Eureka.

51
00:03:02.444 --> 00:03:05.708
The bag and the null are simply left out.

52
00:03:06.633 --> 00:03:07.519
Let's map it.

53
00:03:07.519 --> 00:03:09.563
The torn-off label is erasure.

54
00:03:09.563 --> 00:03:12.969
The note is the real class, written into the copy.

55
00:03:12.969 --> 00:03:17.193
Pinning the note to the job is inline: one copy for each call.

56
00:03:17.193 --> 00:03:21.280
And reified is the clerk's habit of copying the label first.

57
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.806
Now let's be skeptical.

58
00:03:23.806 --> 00:03:28.622
If inline already copies the body into the caller, isn't that enough?

59
00:03:28.622 --> 00:03:29.949
Why the extra word?

60
00:03:29.949 --> 00:03:33.020
Remove reified, keep inline, and compile it.

61
00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:34.137
Pause and guess.

62
00:03:37.333 --> 00:03:41.511
It doesn't compile: cannot check for instance of erased type T.

63
00:03:41.511 --> 00:03:45.026
Copying the body isn't the same as copying the label.

64
00:03:45.026 --> 00:03:49.137
Without reified, the copy still says T, and T is still erased.

65
00:03:49.137 --> 00:03:53.381
So you need both: a copy for each call, and a note on each copy.

66
00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.949
The other rules follow from the picture.

67
00:03:56.949 --> 00:03:58.473
Reified without inline?

68
00:03:58.473 --> 00:04:02.181
Only type parameters of inline functions can be reified.

69
00:04:02.181 --> 00:04:05.228
That's a clerk with no job to pin the note to.

70
00:04:05.433 --> 00:04:07.246
And this one matters most.

71
00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:12.477
A generic function with an ordinary T tries to pass it along: x dot isA&lt;T&gt;.

72
00:04:12.477 --> 00:04:16.103
Kotlin says: cannot use T as reified type parameter.

73
00:04:16.103 --> 00:04:17.498
Use a class instead.

74
00:04:17.498 --> 00:04:23.216
That caller's own label is already gone, so there's nothing to copy onto the note.

75
00:04:23.433 --> 00:04:25.708
So the requirement spreads upward.

76
00:04:25.708 --> 00:04:32.129
The caller must be inline with a reified T too, or it must pass a real class, the way Java does.

77
00:04:33.033 --> 00:04:35.576
Now the names, in one breath each.

78
00:04:35.576 --> 00:04:40.213
inline copies a function's body into each place that calls it.

79
00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:44.252
reified writes the caller's real class into that copy.

80
00:04:44.252 --> 00:04:49.487
Inside, you get back what erasure took away: is T, as T, and T::class.

81
00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.806
And the guardrail: the note has room for one word.

82
00:04:53.806 --> 00:05:02.797
filterIsInstance of List&lt;String&gt; keeps every list, even a list of weights, because after inlining the check is only instanceof List.

83
00:05:02.797 --> 00:05:04.636
The demo prints both lists.

84
00:05:04.636 --> 00:05:09.200
So use reified for the outer class: checks, casts and class tokens.

85
00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:11.628
So, where did Apple go?

86
00:05:11.628 --> 00:05:14.749
The compiler saw it, checked it, and erased it.

87
00:05:14.749 --> 00:05:21.391
Make the function inline and T reified, and the compiler copies the label onto a note at every call.

88
00:05:21.391 --> 00:05:24.313
Ask T what it is, and it can finally answer.

89
00:05:24.533 --> 00:05:25.389
Quick check.

90
00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:30.882
nameOf is inline, with a reified T, and returns the simple name of T's class.

91
00:05:30.882 --> 00:05:33.023
What does nameOf&lt;Lemon&gt; print?

92
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:36.611
Lemon.

93
00:05:36.611 --> 00:05:41.205
The note at that call says Lemon, and the demo prints exactly that.
